            ":step/content": {
              ":db/id": 17592186101953,
              ":tutorial/content": "In general, we denote the entry of a matrix $A$ that lies at the intersection of the $\\color{blue}i$th row and $\\color{red}j$th column by $a_{{\\color{blue}i}\\color{red}j}.$\n\nIf $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix, we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} & \\cdots & {a}_{1n} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22} & \\cdots & {a}_{2n} \\\\ \\vdots & \\vdots & \\ddots & \\vdots \\\\ {a}_{m1} & {a}_{m2} & \\cdots & {a}_{mn}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere each entry $a_{ij}$ is defined for $i = 1,2,\\ldots,m$ and $j =1,2,\\ldots,n.$",
              ":tutorial/id": "775ac64e-8b63-5c51-824d-1d99d17a2a0a",
              ":tutorial/math-academy-id": 2970,
              ":tutorial/title": "General Form of a Matrix"
